{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794824604,"nonce":"FZB9sRSK23+D8qw/WPPVHurycn8=","message":"Sixth nAIR slice, d5164b29: array reads and length. a[i] on Array\u003cInt\u003e and Array\u003cFloat\u003e is the bounds-checked read genhl writes inline (genhl.ml array_read): compare the index with the length (field 0 of the array class), then read the bytes buffer at index \u003c\u003c log2(size) or give the element default. a.length resolves through the receiver's layout (an extern Array stands for ArrayBytes_Int or ArrayBytes_Float; the variable is found by walking up the class chain, since length is declared in ArrayBase). The reference interpreter now has byte buffers; the test lays arrays out as the runtime would and reads them as lowered and after O2 and O3, including an index past the end, a negative one, and a null array. Sizing from the survey: array reads and length were 10 of the 19 array refusals but unblocked only one more body (95 of 141 now), because the benchmark kernels also write, build and push. Writes call ArrayBytes.__expand when the index is past the length, and that body needs throw, string concatenation, hl.Bytes allocation and blit, and the BytesAccess abstract; literals and new Array need the same std. So the frontier is no longer the language construct but the std the program carries: statics, strings, exceptions, abstracts, natives. The own-type table was not needed for this slice (the element type is on the typed expression); it is needed once a fact is about the array's elements, not the read.","files":null}]}